**Big discovery — and this changes the whole picture.** That instance's `TIMEOUT` isn't a capacity or health-check-tuning issue. Look at the actual boot log:

```
CommandException: No URLs matched: gs://cn-staging-pg-ae-n-app-125609/releases/backend/latest.txt
Script "startup-script" failed with error: exit status 1
```

**Root cause:** `cn-backend-template-v5` — the template every instance in both `cn-backend-mig` and `cn-backend-mig-regional` is using — has a **startup script that downloads the app from GCS at boot time** (the old deployment method your migration was replacing). It expects a file at `gs://cn-staging-pg-ae-n-app-125609/releases/backend/latest.txt`.

**That file no longer exists — because earlier tonight, I had you remove the `Package Release` / `Upload Release to GCS` steps from `cloudbuild.yaml`**, since we assumed they were legacy and unused. That assumption was wrong for `cn-backend-template-v5`: it still actively depends on that GCS artifact existing. So every *new* instance created from `v5` now fails to boot the app correctly — this isn't a fluke, it will happen to every single new instance from this template, permanently, until either the GCS artifact comes back or we stop using this template.

**This also means:** the two "healthy" instances you have right now in `cn-backend-mig` and most of `cn-backend-mig-regional` are healthy only because they were created *before* we removed those steps — they still have the old release cached locally from before. Any instance replaced or newly created from now on **will fail the same way**.

---

## The actual correct fix

**Stop using `cn-backend-template-v5` entirely, for anything, going forward.** The right template is the one Packer built tonight — `cn-backend-template-fd726a3` — which has the app baked directly into the image (no GCS fetch needed at boot, which was the whole point of tonight's migration).

**Immediate action: point the regional MIG at the correct new template:**
```bash
gcloud compute instance-groups managed set-instance-template cn-backend-mig-regional \
  --project=pg-ae-n-app-125609 \
  --region=me-central2 \
  --template=cn-backend-template-fd726a3
```

**Then roll existing instances onto it:**
```bash
gcloud compute instance-groups managed rolling-action start-update cn-backend-mig-regional \
  --project=pg-ae-n-app-125609 \
  --region=me-central2 \
  --version=template=cn-backend-template-fd726a3 \
  --max-surge=1 --max-unavailable=0
```

⚠️ **Also note:** that boot log shows the instance is running a **GitHub Actions self-hosted runner**, **Splunk forwarder**, and **Microsoft Defender (mdatp)** — meaning `cn-backend-template-v5` was cloned from a fuller, more complex VM setup than what our clean Packer image builds. Your new golden image doesn't include these. Confirm whether the self-hosted runner / Splunk / Defender agents are actually required on these app servers, or whether they were incidental leftovers from how the original VM was provisioned — this affects whether the new template needs additional provisioners added, or whether it's correctly lean as-is.

**Run the two commands above, then recheck instance health:**
```bash
gcloud compute instance-groups managed list-instances cn-backend-mig-regional \
  --project=pg-ae-n-app-125609 \
  --region=me-central2
```

Paste that — this should now show instances booting cleanly with the app already present, no GCS dependency, no timeout.
